<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Hadejia Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1808 CE – 1906 CE (approx.) · capital Hadejia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A north-eastern emirate of the Sokoto Caliphate, remembered for the rebel emir Buhari and for being one of the last emirates to fight the British.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadejia">Hadejia</a> arose from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> in the borderlands between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausaland</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> clans took control of the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> town of Hadejia around 1808. Its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> received their authority from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>, the Kanuri-speaking local populations and their neighbours in Katagum.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadejia">Hadejia</a> lay on the fertile floodplains of the Hadejia river, farming grain and raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. The emir ruled through titled officials. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Buhari, who reigned in 1848-1850 and again from 1851 to 1863, Hadejia rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>, defeated punitive expeditions and raided its neighbours, before returning to allegiance after his death.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadejia">Hadejia</a> submitted to the British in 1903 but resisted their authority, and in 1906 British troops stormed the town; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Muhammadu was killed. It then became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>, and the Emir of Hadejia still reigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigawa-state">Jigawa State</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hadejia-emirate.jpg" alt="Hadejia Emirate" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: Sani Ahmad Usman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Another_site_of_River_Hadejia_Jigawa_state_Nigeria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1808 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hadejia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Buhari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Fulfulde, Hausa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Hadejia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1857&amp;focus=hadejia-emirate" aria-label="Hadejia Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4956 1714 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hadejia Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5276 1970L5276 1970M5276 1970L5144 1947M5276 1970L5170 2068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5276 1970h0M5144 1947h0M5170 2068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5276 1970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5276 1970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1857&amp;focus=hadejia-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
